L = lesson(ch, "tectonics", "Plate tectonics and plate boundaries", 
    ["Describe the structure of the Earth and the movement of plates", "Distinguish constructive, destructive and conservative boundaries",
     "Link boundaries to landforms, earthquakes and volcanoes"],
    [("definition", "Plates", "The **lithosphere** (crust and top of the mantle) is broken into rigid **plates**. They move slowly, a few centimetres a year, over the hotter, softer **asthenosphere**. Causes include convection currents in the mantle, **ridge push** and **slab pull** (a cold, dense plate sinking pulls the rest of the plate with it)."),
     ("retenir", "Three kinds of boundary", "**Constructive (divergent):** plates move apart, magma rises, new crust forms (mid-ocean ridges, rift valleys). **Destructive (convergent):** plates move together; a dense oceanic plate is **subducted** (trenches, fold mountains, volcanoes, strong earthquakes). **Conservative (transform):** plates slide past each other; earthquakes but no crust created or destroyed."),
     ("pieges", "Common mistakes", "- Volcanoes and earthquakes are not only found at plate edges: hot spots and other zones exist.\n- Mount Cameroon lies on the Cameroon Volcanic Line, which is not a simple plate boundary and whose origin is still discussed by geologists.\n- Plates move, continents do not ‘float’ separately from them.")],
    [("Example 1 — Identify the boundary", "Plate A (oceanic) and plate B (continental) are moving towards each other. Name the boundary, the process and two landforms or hazards.",
      ["Plates moving together form a destructive boundary.", "The denser oceanic plate A sinks beneath B: this is subduction.", "Landforms: an ocean trench and fold mountains or a chain of volcanoes on plate B.", "Hazards: powerful earthquakes and explosive eruptions."],
      "Destructive boundary; subduction; trench, volcanoes/fold mountains; earthquakes and eruptions."),
     ("Example 2 — Explain a rift valley", "East Africa has long, steep-sided valleys with lakes and volcanoes. Explain how plate movement can produce them.",
      ["Where the crust is pulled apart (a divergent setting), it is stretched and thinned.", "Blocks of crust between faults drop down and form a rift valley with steep sides.", "Magma rises through the weak crust and builds volcanoes.", "Shallow earthquakes occur as the faults move."],
      "Stretching and faulting drop blocks to form the valley; rising magma gives volcanoes and earthquakes.")],
    [M("Which boundary creates new crust?", "Constructive", ["Destructive", "Conservative", "Collision of two continents"], "New crust forms where plates move apart and magma rises."),
     TF("Subduction happens at constructive boundaries.", False, "Subduction occurs at destructive boundaries, where one plate sinks under another."),
     MT("Match each boundary type to a typical feature.", [("Constructive", "Mid-ocean ridge"), ("Destructive", "Ocean trench"), ("Conservative", "Transform fault"), ("Continental collision", "Fold mountain chain")],
        "Ridges form where plates diverge, trenches where oceanic crust is subducted, transform faults where plates slide past, fold mountains where continents collide."),
     M("Which of these is NOT a mechanism driving plate movement?", "The Moon’s phases", ["Slab pull", "Ridge push", "Mantle convection"], "Plate motion is driven by mantle convection, slab pull and ridge push; the phases of the Moon play no part."),
     O("People still live close to active volcanoes. Give three reasons.", "Volcanic soils are often very fertile, so farming is productive. Geothermal energy and minerals can be exploited. Volcanoes attract tourists and give jobs. Families also have land, homes and social ties there and may lack the means to move.",
       "Award one mark per reason (fertile soil, energy/minerals, tourism/jobs, attachment/lack of alternatives), maximum 3."),
     O("Describe and explain the features found at a destructive plate boundary where oceanic crust meets continental crust.",
       "The denser oceanic plate is subducted beneath the continental plate, forming a deep ocean trench. Friction and melting produce magma, which rises to form volcanoes on the continental plate. Compression crumples sediments into fold mountains. The sticking and sudden slipping of the plates causes strong earthquakes, some deep. Subduction destroys oceanic crust.",
       "Trench and subduction explained (1); magma/volcanoes (1); fold mountains (1); earthquakes with cause (1).")],
    [("Which layer of the Earth is broken into plates?", "The lithosphere", ["The outer core", "The inner core", "The atmosphere"], "The lithosphere (crust and uppermost mantle) is divided into plates."),
     ("A mid-ocean ridge is found at a", "Constructive boundary", ["Destructive boundary", "Conservative boundary", "Hot spring only"], "Ridges mark places where plates diverge."),
     ("Fold mountains are best linked to", "Compression where plates collide", ["Plates sliding past", "Plates moving apart", "River erosion only"], "Collision and compression crumple rock layers."),
     ("Slab pull is", "The pull of a sinking plate on the rest of the plate", ["A wave type in earthquakes", "A volcanic gas", "A type of fold"], "The sinking edge is cold and dense and drags the plate."),
     ("A transform fault is where plates", "Slide past one another", ["Move apart", "Collide head on", "Melt completely"], "Conservative boundaries have sideways movement.")],
    (plates_fig, "The three types of plate boundary (schematic).", "Three small diagrams with arrows: constructive boundary with plates moving apart, destructive with plates moving together, conservative with plates sliding past."),
    ["Plate speeds are given only as ‘a few centimetres a year’; check the figure used in the official syllabus.", "Origin of the Cameroon Volcanic Line is debated: confirm how the syllabus presents it."])

