L = lesson(ch, "weathering", "Weathering and mass movement",
    ["Distinguish physical, chemical and biological weathering", "Explain why chemical weathering dominates in the humid tropics", "Describe types of mass movement and the factors that trigger them"],
    [("definition", "Weathering", "**Weathering** is the breakdown of rock **in place**, with no transport. **Erosion** involves a moving agent (water, wind, ice). **Physical** weathering breaks rock without changing its minerals (heating and cooling, freeze-thaw, salt crystals). **Chemical** weathering changes minerals (carbonation, hydrolysis, oxidation). **Biological** weathering is caused by roots, burrowing animals and lichens."),
     ("retenir", "Weathering in warm, wet climates", "Chemical reactions speed up with heat and water, so in the humid tropics rocks decay to great depths, forming thick weathered layers and clay-rich soils. In hot deserts temperature changes and salt crystals dominate. Freeze-thaw needs repeated frost, so it is rare in the lowland tropics."),
     ("retenir", "Mass movement", "**Mass movement** is the downslope movement of weathered material under gravity. It ranges from slow **soil creep** to **mudflows**, **slumps**, **landslides** and **rockfalls**. Triggers: steep slope, heavy rain that saturates soil, loss of vegetation, undercutting by rivers or roads, and earthquakes."),
     ("attention", "Weathering is not erosion", "Do not write that rivers ‘weather’ the valley: rivers erode. Landslides have occurred on steep, built-up hillsides in Cameroon during heavy rain, so slope management matters for planning.")],
    [("Example 1 — Name the type", "Rock containing limestone is dissolved slowly by rainwater that has absorbed carbon dioxide. Name the process and the group of weathering it belongs to.",
      ["Rainwater with dissolved CO₂ is a weak acid.", "It reacts with calcium carbonate: this is carbonation.", "A chemical change takes place, so it is chemical weathering."], "Carbonation (chemical weathering)."),
     ("Example 2 — Explain a landslide", "A steep hillside was cleared of trees for housing. After days of rain a section slid downhill. Explain why.",
      ["Roots held the soil together and trees intercepted rain; both were lost.", "Rainfall filled the soil pores, adding weight and reducing friction between particles.", "On a steep slope gravity then overcame the strength of the material.", "Cutting into the slope for houses removed support at the base."],
      "Loss of vegetation, saturation, steepness and undercutting triggered the movement.")],
    [M("Which is an example of biological weathering?", "Tree roots widening a crack in rock", ["Rain dissolving limestone", "Rock splitting after daily heating", "A river carrying pebbles"], "Plants and animals act mechanically or chemically on rock."),
     TF("Freeze-thaw weathering is the dominant process in the lowland equatorial forest.", False, "It needs temperatures that fall below and rise above 0°C repeatedly; lowland equatorial areas do not freeze."),
     MT("Match the process to its type.", [("Oxidation of iron minerals", "Chemical"), ("Salt crystals prising grains apart", "Physical"), ("Lichen acids attacking rock", "Biological"), ("Slow downslope shuffle of soil", "Mass movement")],
        "Oxidation is chemical; salt crystallisation physical; lichens biological; soil creep is a mass movement, not weathering."),
     M("Why is chemical weathering fast in the humid tropics?", "High temperature and plentiful water speed up reactions", ["Frost is frequent", "Wind is always strong", "There is little vegetation"], "Warm, wet conditions favour chemical reactions."),
     O("Explain two ways in which human activity can increase the risk of landslides.", "Removing trees takes away roots and shelter from rain, so soil is weaker and wetter. Cutting slopes for roads and houses removes support. Poor drainage allows water to soak into the slope. Building heavy structures on steep ground adds load.",
       "One mark for each way and one for explaining the link, maximum 4 (two ways x 2)."),
     O("Describe the main types of mass movement and explain the factors that cause them.", "Mass movements include creep (slow), mudflow (wet, fluid), slump and landslide (blocks of material sliding) and rockfall (free fall from cliffs). They happen where gravity overcomes resistance. Steepness increases the downslope force. Water adds weight and lowers friction. Vegetation loss, undercutting and earthquakes can trigger movement. Weak, weathered rock fails more easily.",
       "Types described (2); factors named (2); links explained (1).")],
    [("Weathering differs from erosion because weathering", "Takes place without transport of material", ["Needs a river", "Only happens in deserts", "Only happens at night"], "Erosion needs a moving agent."),
     ("Carbonation mainly attacks", "Limestone", ["Granite quartz", "Sand grains only", "Volcanic glass only"], "Calcium carbonate dissolves in weak carbonic acid."),
     ("Soil creep is", "A very slow downslope movement of soil", ["A rapid avalanche of rock", "A type of river flow", "A volcanic hazard"], "It is the slowest mass movement."),
     ("A common trigger for landslides is", "Saturation of soil by heavy rain", ["A dry season", "Low slope angle", "Dense root cover"], "Water adds weight and lowers friction."),
     ("Red colour in tropical soils is mostly due to", "Iron oxides", ["Salt", "Coal", "Limestone"], "Oxidised iron gives red and yellow tones.")],
    (slope_fig, "Slope processes: steeper slopes favour rapid movement.", "Slope profile showing a steep upper part with rockfalls and landslides and a gentle lower part with soil creep."),
    ["Deeply weathered tropical profiles: check how deeply the syllabus expects students to describe them.", "Landslide risk statement for Cameroon is general; choose a local case study with the teacher."])

