L = lesson(ch, "rivers", "River processes, landforms and flooding",
    ["Describe erosion, transport and deposition by rivers", "Link landforms to the upper, middle and lower course", "Interpret a storm hydrograph and explain flood risk"],
    [("definition", "Processes", "**Erosion:** hydraulic action, abrasion, attrition and solution. **Transport:** solution, suspension, saltation and traction (rolling). **Deposition** happens when velocity falls, for example at a bend, in a lake or at the mouth. Larger load needs faster flow; flow is fastest in flood."),
     ("retenir", "Landforms along the long profile", "**Upper course:** steep, V-shaped valley, rapids, waterfalls, interlocking spurs. **Middle course:** wider valley, meanders with erosion on the outside bend and deposition on the inside. **Lower course:** flat floodplain, levees, oxbow lakes, and a **delta** or estuary at the mouth. Rivers such as the Sanaga and Wouri drain Cameroon to the Atlantic."),
     ("methode", "Reading a hydrograph", "A **storm hydrograph** plots river discharge against time after rain. **Lag time** is the time between peak rainfall and peak discharge. A short lag time and steep rising limb mean a flashy, flood-prone river: steep slopes, impermeable rock, saturated soil, little vegetation and urban surfaces all shorten the lag."),
     ("attention", "Mistakes to avoid", "- A meander bend is eroded on the outside and has deposition on the inside, not the reverse.\n- An oxbow lake is a cut-off meander, not a type of delta.\n- Lag time is peak rainfall to peak discharge, not the length of the whole storm.")],
    [("Example 1 — Lag time", "Peak rainfall was at 10:00 and peak discharge at 16:00. Find the lag time. A town stream peaks 2 hours after rain; say which is flashier and why.",
      ["Lag time = 16:00 − 10:00 = 6 hours.", "The town stream has a lag of 2 hours, which is shorter.", "Concrete and drains carry water quickly to the channel: little infiltration."],
      "6 hours; the urban stream is flashier because of impermeable surfaces."),
     ("Example 2 — Explain an oxbow lake", "Describe how an oxbow lake forms.",
      ["Erosion on the outside of a meander and deposition on the inside make the loop narrower.", "In a flood the river cuts through the neck of the loop.", "Deposition seals the ends of the old loop.", "The cut-off loop remains as a curved lake."],
      "A meander neck is cut through in flood and the abandoned loop is sealed off.")],
    [M("Which landform is typical of the upper course?", "Waterfall", ["Delta", "Oxbow lake", "Wide floodplain"], "Steep upper courses have waterfalls and rapids."),
     N("Peak rainfall is at 08:00 and peak discharge at 14:30. What is the lag time in hours?", 6.5, "14:30 − 08:00 = 6 hours 30 minutes = 6.5 hours.", 0.01, "hours"),
     TF("Deposition occurs mainly where river velocity increases.", False, "Deposition occurs where velocity falls, such as inside meander bends or at the mouth."),
     M("Which factor would SHORTEN lag time?", "Urban tarmac and drains", ["Dense forest", "Gentle slopes with deep soil", "A large lake upstream"], "Impermeable surfaces send water quickly to the river."),
     MT("Match term and description.", [("Levee", "Raised bank built by flood deposits"), ("Delta", "Deposit at a river mouth"), ("Meander", "Bend in the river channel"), ("Traction", "Rolling of large stones along the bed")],
        "Each term has a standard definition."),
     O("Explain why flooding is a growing problem in many tropical towns, and suggest two ways to reduce it.", "Towns grow over floodplains and cover soil with concrete, so rain runs off quickly and drains block with waste. Removing vegetation also increases run-off. Solutions: keep drainage channels clear and enlarged; stop building in flood-prone areas and protect wetlands; plant trees; give early warnings.",
       "Causes (2); two valid measures (2); development of at least one point (1).")],
    [("The river’s ability to carry load is greatest", "In flood", ["In drought", "At the source only", "When velocity is lowest"], "High velocity and discharge mean higher capacity."),
     ("Erosion by the river using stones in its load is", "Abrasion", ["Solution", "Attrition", "Saltation"], "Abrasion is the scraping of bed and banks by load."),
     ("A floodplain is found mainly in the", "Lower course", ["Upper course", "Source", "Glacier"], "Deposition builds a wide, flat floodplain."),
     ("Lag time is measured between", "Peak rainfall and peak discharge", ["Start and end of rain", "Source and mouth", "Flood and drought"], "It indicates how fast a river responds."),
     ("Which gives a longer lag time?", "Forested, gently sloping basin", ["Paved urban basin", "Bare steep slopes", "Saturated clay"], "Vegetation and infiltration delay run-off.")],
    (river_fig, "A river’s long profile is steep near the source and gentle near the mouth.", "A curved line falling steeply then flattening, divided into upper, middle and lower course."),
    ["Hydrograph reading skills: confirm whether the syllabus expects drainage-basin systems diagrams.", "Case studies of local rivers (Wouri, Sanaga, Benue) to be chosen by the teacher; no statistics given."])

