ch = p.chapter("climate", "Climatology and biogeography")
belts_fig = table(["Latitude", "Pressure", "Surface wind"], [["Equator", "Low (ITCZ)", "Light, variable"], ["About 30°", "High", "Trades and westerlies"], ["About 60°", "Low", "Westerlies meet polar air"], ["Poles", "High", "Polar easterlies"]], 440, 12, 32)
L = lesson(ch, "atmosphere", "Atmosphere, heat budget and global winds",
    ["Describe the composition and layers of the atmosphere", "Explain why temperature varies with latitude and altitude", "Describe pressure belts and the main surface winds"],
    [("definition", "Atmosphere and layers", "The atmosphere is mostly nitrogen (about 78%) and oxygen (about 21%), with small amounts of argon, carbon dioxide, water vapour and other gases. Layers from the ground: **troposphere** (weather occurs here), **stratosphere** (ozone layer), **mesosphere**, **thermosphere**. In the troposphere temperature falls with height, on average about 6.5°C per 1000 m."),
     ("retenir", "Why latitude matters", "Near the equator the Sun’s rays are nearly overhead: they strike a small area and pass through less atmosphere, so heating is intense. Towards the poles the same energy is spread over a larger area and passes through more air. Surfaces with high **albedo** (ice, bright sand) reflect more energy than dark forest."),
     ("retenir", "Pressure belts and winds", "Heating at the equator makes air rise (low pressure). It sinks near 30° latitude (high pressure, deserts). Winds blow from high to low pressure but are deflected by the **Coriolis effect**: to the right in the northern hemisphere, to the left in the south. This gives the **trade winds** (north-east in the north) and the **westerlies**."),
     ("pieges", "Common confusions", "- Winds are named after where they come FROM: a north-east trade wind comes from the north-east.\n- Warm air rises and so gives LOW surface pressure; sinking air gives HIGH pressure.\n- Weather occurs in the troposphere, not in the ozone layer.")],
    [("Example 1 — Temperature and height", "A town at sea level has an average temperature of 28°C. A hill town 1000 m higher is in the same area. Estimate its temperature (use 6.5°C per 1000 m).",
      ["Temperature fall = 6.5 × (1000 ÷ 1000) = 6.5°C.", "Hill town ≈ 28 − 6.5 = 21.5°C."], "About 21.5°C."),
     ("Example 2 — Direction of a trade wind", "Explain why the wind at the surface between 30°N and the equator blows from the north-east.",
      ["Air moves from the high-pressure belt near 30°N towards the equatorial low, that is from north to south.", "The Coriolis effect deflects moving air to the right in the northern hemisphere.", "A southward wind deflected to the right becomes a wind from the north-east."], "Pressure gradient plus Coriolis deflection gives the north-east trades.")],
    [N("A station at sea level is 30°C. Using 6.5°C per 1000 m, what is the temperature at 2000 m?", 17, "30 − (6.5 × 2) = 17°C.", 0.1, "°C"),
     M("Which layer contains the ozone layer?", "Stratosphere", ["Troposphere", "Thermosphere", "Mesosphere"], "The ozone layer is in the stratosphere."),
     TF("Air at the equatorial low-pressure belt is mostly rising.", True, "Intense heating makes air rise, producing low surface pressure."),
     M("Why are the equatorial regions warmer than the poles?", "Sun’s rays are nearly vertical and pass through less atmosphere", ["They are closer to the Sun by thousands of kilometres", "They have no clouds", "They have no oceans"], "Angle of the rays and path length are the main reasons."),
     MT("Match each wind to its description.", [("Trade winds", "Blow towards the equator from the subtropical highs"), ("Westerlies", "Blow from the subtropical highs towards 60° latitude"), ("Coriolis effect", "Deflects winds right in the northern hemisphere"), ("Albedo", "Reflectivity of a surface")], "Standard definitions."),
     O("Explain why many of the world’s great deserts lie near latitude 30° north and south.", "Air rises at the equator, loses moisture through heavy rain and spreads out at height. It cools and sinks near 30°, forming a belt of high pressure. Sinking air warms and becomes drier, so clouds do not form and rainfall is low. Skies are clear so days are very hot. This explains the Sahara.",
       "Rising air at equator (1); descent near 30° (1); warming/drying of sinking air (1); result: little cloud and rain (1).")],
    [("About what share of dry air is nitrogen?", "78%", ["21%", "50%", "1%"], "Nitrogen is the largest component."),
     ("Weather happens mostly in the", "Troposphere", ["Stratosphere", "Thermosphere", "Mesosphere"], "Clouds and rain form in the lowest layer."),
     ("A wind from the south-west is called", "A south-westerly", ["A north-easterly", "An easterly", "A trade wind from the north-east"], "Winds take the name of the direction they come from."),
     ("The Coriolis effect deflects moving air to the right in the", "Northern hemisphere", ["Southern hemisphere", "Both hemispheres", "Neither"], "It reverses in the south."),
     ("High albedo surfaces", "Reflect much incoming radiation", ["Absorb most radiation", "Create tropical heat", "Reduce pressure"], "Ice and bright sand reflect.")],
    (belts_fig, "Pressure belts and the main surface winds.", "A table of four latitude zones with the usual pressure and wind pattern."),
    ["Check the syllabus convention for pressure-belt latitudes (30° and 60° are used here as approximate values).", "6.5°C per 1000 m is the standard average lapse rate; use the value the syllabus teaches."])

itcz_fig = flow(["Overhead Sun moves north (June to August)", "ITCZ moves north with it", "South-west monsoon brings rain", "Sun moves south: dry harmattan returns"], 440, "blue", 13, 380)
L = lesson(ch, "tropical", "The ITCZ, the monsoon and tropical climates",
    ["Explain the movement of the ITCZ", "Describe the West African monsoon and the harmattan", "Compare equatorial and tropical continental climates and relate them to Cameroon"],
    [("definition", "ITCZ", "The **Inter-Tropical Convergence Zone (ITCZ)** is the belt where the trade winds of the two hemispheres meet and air rises, forming thick clouds and heavy rain. It follows the overhead Sun with a delay: it moves north from about March to August and south for the rest of the year."),
     ("retenir", "West African monsoon", "When the ITCZ is far north, moist **south-westerly** air from the Atlantic reaches West and Central Africa and brings the rainy season. When it moves south, dry **north-easterly** air from the Sahara dominates: the **harmattan**, a dry, dusty wind in the dry season (roughly November to March)."),
     ("retenir", "Climate types", "**Equatorial:** hot all year, small annual temperature range, heavy rain in every month, daily convectional storms. **Tropical continental (savanna):** hot, a longer dry season and one wet season, a larger temperature range. In Cameroon the south has a long, humid, mostly bimodal rain pattern; the north has one rainy season and a long dry season; the Atlantic coast and the slopes of Mount Cameroon are extremely wet."),
     ("attention", "Do not confuse", "- Rainfall total is not the same as temperature range: range = highest monthly mean − lowest monthly mean.\n- The ITCZ is a belt of low pressure and rising air, not a single line fixed on the equator.\n- Relief rain on mountains adds to the monsoon effect: windward slopes are much wetter than leeward ones.")],
    [("Example 1 — Temperature range", "A station’s hottest month averages 31°C and its coolest month 24°C. Another station has 27°C and 26°C. Which is more likely to be equatorial and why?",
      ["Range of station 1 = 31 − 24 = 7°C.", "Range of station 2 = 27 − 26 = 1°C.", "Equatorial climates have very small ranges, so station 2 is more likely equatorial."],
      "Station 2 (range 1°C)."),
     ("Example 2 — Why is Maroua drier than Douala?", "Explain in simple terms why the far north of Cameroon gets less rain than the coast.",
      ["The coast is close to the moist Atlantic air and the ITCZ stays near for long.", "The far north is far from the ocean and the ITCZ reaches it late and leaves early.", "So the northern rainy season is short and the dry harmattan season is long."], "Distance from the moist air source and a short ITCZ stay give the north less rain.")],
    [M("The harmattan is a", "Dry, dusty north-easterly wind", ["Wet south-westerly wind", "Cold ocean current", "Type of cyclone"], "It blows from the Sahara."),
     N("A station’s warmest month is 29°C and its coolest 23°C. What is the annual temperature range?", 6, "29 − 23 = 6°C.", 0, "°C"),
     TF("The ITCZ is at its most northern position in about July–August.", True, "It follows the overhead Sun with a lag."),
     M("Which feature best describes an equatorial climate?", "Rain in every month and a small temperature range", ["A long dry season and large range", "Rain only in winter", "Freezing nights"], "Equatorial climates are hot and wet all year."),
     M("Why are windward slopes of Mount Cameroon very wet?", "Moist air is forced to rise and cool, forming relief rain", ["They are far from the ocean", "They lie in a rain shadow", "They are in the Sahara"], "Orographic uplift gives heavy rain."),
     O("Describe how the movement of the ITCZ explains the wet and dry seasons in northern Cameroon.", "When the overhead Sun and the ITCZ move north, moist south-westerly air spreads over the north, giving the wet season (roughly May to September). When the ITCZ retreats south, dry north-easterly harmattan air returns, giving a long dry season. The wet season is short because the ITCZ is overhead only briefly at that latitude.",
       "ITCZ moves with the Sun (1); moist air and wet season (1); dry air and harmattan (1); shorter wet season in the north (1).")],
    [("The ITCZ is a zone where", "Trade winds converge and air rises", ["Air sinks and clears the sky", "Polar air meets tropical air", "Cold ocean water rises"], "Convergence and uplift give cloud and rain."),
     ("Moist air in the West African monsoon comes from the", "South-west, Atlantic Ocean", ["North-east, Sahara", "East, Indian Ocean", "North, Mediterranean"], "The monsoon is a south-westerly flow."),
     ("A large annual temperature range is typical of", "Tropical continental climates", ["Equatorial climates", "Mountain tops only", "Oceans"], "Dry and wet seasons give a bigger range."),
     ("Convectional rain in the tropics is caused by", "Strong heating and rising air", ["Cold fronts", "Ocean currents only", "Mountains only"], "Heated ground produces thunderstorms."),
     ("In which months is the harmattan strongest in Central Africa?", "In the dry season, roughly November to March", ["June to August", "All year", "Only in April"], "It blows when the ITCZ is south.")],
    (itcz_fig, "How the ITCZ produces seasons.", "A chain of four boxes showing the Sun moving north, the ITCZ moving north, the monsoon rains and the return of the dry harmattan."),
    ["Months for Cameroon’s seasons and the bimodal southern rain pattern are approximate: verify against the syllabus and local climate data.", "Mount Cameroon’s heavy rainfall is stated in qualitative terms only; add exact values from an approved source if needed."])

