L = lesson(ch, "soils", "Soils, vegetation and biomes",
    ["Describe a soil profile and the factors of soil formation", "Explain the characteristics of tropical soils", "Describe tropical rainforest and savanna vegetation and their adaptations"],
    [("definition", "Soil and its profile", "**Soil** is a mixture of weathered mineral particles, organic matter, water and air. A **soil profile** has horizons: **O** (litter and humus), **A** (topsoil), **B** (subsoil where leached minerals collect), **C** (weathered parent rock) and **R** (bedrock). Soil forms from parent material, climate, relief, organisms and time."),
     ("retenir", "Tropical soils", "In hot, wet forests chemical weathering and heavy rain wash out (**leach**) soluble minerals, leaving iron and aluminium oxides: red, deep, acidic soils (**ferralsols**, often called laterite when hardened). Fertility is low in the soil itself: most nutrients are held in the vegetation and recycled quickly from leaf litter. Clear the forest and the nutrients are soon lost."),
     ("retenir", "Vegetation", "**Tropical rainforest:** tall evergreen trees in layers (emergent, canopy, understorey, floor), buttress roots, drip-tip leaves, high biodiversity. **Savanna:** grassland with scattered fire- and drought-resistant trees such as the baobab, a long dry season, deep roots. **Sahel:** thorny scrub and sparse grass."),
     ("attention", "Fertile does not mean tropical", "Young volcanic soils, such as those around Mount Cameroon and the volcanic highlands of western Cameroon, are generally richer in minerals than old leached soils. Do not say that all tropical soils are infertile.")],
    [("Example 1 — Why shifting cultivation?", "Explain why farmers using shifting cultivation move to a new plot after a few years.",
      ["Clearing and burning releases a short burst of nutrients into the soil.", "Crops remove nutrients and heavy rain leaches them.", "Yields fall, so the plot is left to fallow and vegetation regrows while the farmer clears elsewhere."],
      "Nutrients are quickly used and leached, so land is rested."),
     ("Example 2 — Adaptation", "Give two adaptations of savanna trees and explain them.",
      ["Thick bark resists grass fires.", "Deep roots reach water during the long dry season.", "Some lose leaves in the dry season to cut water loss."],
      "Thick bark (fire) and deep roots or leaf loss (drought).")],
    [M("Which horizon is the topsoil?", "A", ["B", "C", "R"], "A is the dark layer rich in organic matter."),
     TF("In tropical rainforest most nutrients are stored in the soil rather than in the vegetation.", False, "Most nutrients are in the living biomass and the litter; the soil is often poor."),
     MT("Match the term to its meaning.", [("Leaching", "Washing out of soluble minerals"), ("Humus", "Decayed organic matter"), ("Parent material", "Rock from which soil forms"), ("Fallow", "Land left to recover")], "Standard soil terms."),
     M("Red colour in many tropical soils comes from", "Oxidised iron", ["Soot", "Sand only", "Salt"], "Iron oxides give red tones."),
     O("Explain why clearing tropical rainforest for farming often leads to falling yields.", "Most nutrients are in the trees and leaf litter. Clearing removes them, and heavy rain leaches the remainder. Without roots and cover the soil is exposed to erosion and baking by the sun. After a few harvests the soil can no longer support crops.",
       "Nutrient store in vegetation (1); leaching (1); erosion/exposure (1); falling fertility (1)."),
     O("Compare tropical rainforest and savanna vegetation, and explain the differences by climate.", "Rainforest has tall evergreen trees in layers, dense canopy and great diversity, because rain falls in all months and it is hot all year. Savanna has grass with scattered trees that tolerate fire and drought, since there is a long dry season. Savanna trees may drop leaves and have deep roots, thick bark; rainforest leaves have drip tips to shed water.",
       "Rainforest features (1); savanna features (1); climate link for each (2).")],
    [("The horizon of accumulated minerals washed down is", "B", ["O", "A", "R"], "The subsoil collects leached material."),
     ("Leaching is common in", "Hot, wet climates", ["Cold, dry climates", "Deserts", "Polar regions"], "Heavy rain washes out soluble minerals."),
     ("A buttress root helps a tree to", "Stay upright in shallow soil", ["Store water", "Attract insects", "Make fruit"], "It spreads weight."),
     ("Baobab trees are typical of", "Savanna", ["Mangrove swamp", "Polar tundra", "Alpine meadow"], "They store water and tolerate drought."),
     ("Young volcanic soils are usually", "Rich in minerals", ["Always infertile", "Made of pure sand", "Frozen"], "Fresh volcanic material supplies nutrients.")],
    (soil_fig, "A soil profile from litter to bedrock.", "Five stacked boxes labelled O litter and humus, A topsoil, B subsoil, C weathered rock and R bedrock."),
    ["Soil names (ferralsol, laterite) and the nutrient-cycling statement are textbook generalisations: check the terms used in the syllabus.", "The link between volcanic soils and farming around Mount Cameroon is stated qualitatively: verify with a local source."])

