L = lesson(ch, "population", "Population dynamics and policies",
    ["Define and calculate birth rate, death rate and natural increase", "Describe the demographic transition model", "Explain population pyramids and population policies"],
    [("definition", "Key terms", "**Birth rate** and **death rate** are births and deaths per 1000 people per year. **Natural increase** = birth rate − death rate (per 1000; divide by 10 for a percentage). **Fertility rate** is the average number of children per woman. **Life expectancy** is the average years a newborn is expected to live. **Dependency ratio** = (under 15 + 65 and over) ÷ (15 to 64) × 100."),
     ("retenir", "Demographic transition", "**Stage 1:** high birth and death rates, small growth. **Stage 2:** death rate falls (health, food), birth rate stays high: rapid growth, many countries in sub-Saharan Africa are in this stage or moving to stage 3. **Stage 3:** birth rate falls. **Stage 4:** both low, slow growth. Some add a stage 5 with declining population."),
     ("retenir", "Pyramids and policies", "A pyramid with a **wide base** and sharply narrowing top shows high birth rates and a young population. A narrow base shows low birth rates and ageing. **Policies:** anti-natalist (family planning, girls’ education, later marriage), pro-natalist (child allowances) and policies on migration. Policies must respect rights and local cultures."),
     ("pieges", "Frequent errors", "- Natural increase excludes migration.\n- Growth of 26 per 1000 equals 2.6%, not 26%.\n- Population density (people per km²) differs from total population.\n- A young population creates a high dependency ratio because many dependants are children.")],
    [("Example 1 — Natural increase", "A region has a birth rate of 38 per 1000 and a death rate of 12 per 1000. Find the natural increase as a percentage.",
      ["Natural increase = 38 − 12 = 26 per 1000.", "26 ÷ 10 = 2.6%."], "2.6% a year."),
     ("Example 2 — Dependency ratio", "In a country 44% of people are under 15, 53% aged 15 to 64 and 3% aged 65 or more (illustrative figures). Find the dependency ratio.",
      ["Dependants = 44 + 3 = 47%.", "Ratio = 47 ÷ 53 × 100 = 88.7 (to 1 decimal place)."], "About 88.7 dependants per 100 workers.")],
    [N("Birth rate 30 per 1000, death rate 9 per 1000. What is the natural increase in per 1000?", 21, "30 − 9 = 21 per 1000.", 0, "per 1000"),
     M("A pyramid with a very wide base shows", "High birth rate and young population", ["Ageing population", "Low fertility", "Many elderly people"], "Many children relative to adults."),
     TF("Natural increase includes the effect of migration.", False, "Natural increase = births minus deaths only."),
     N("In a country 40% of people are under 15, 56% are 15–64 and 4% are 65 or over (illustrative). Dependency ratio to the nearest whole number?", 79, "(40 + 4) ÷ 56 × 100 = 78.57, about 79.", 0.5),
     M("Which is an anti-natalist measure?", "Providing family planning services and girls’ education", ["Cash payments for each extra child", "Banning contraceptives", "Closing schools"], "Anti-natalist policies aim to lower births, usually voluntarily."),
     O("Explain why the death rate falls before the birth rate in the demographic transition model, and what results.", "Death rates fall as health care, clean water and food supplies improve, which spreads quickly. Birth rates take longer to fall because they depend on family size preferences, education, status of women and economic security, which change slowly. The gap between high births and low deaths causes rapid natural increase and a young population.",
       "Reasons for fall in deaths (1); why births stay high (2); rapid growth/young population (1).")],
    [("Natural increase equals", "Birth rate minus death rate", ["Birth rate plus death rate", "Immigration minus emigration", "Fertility times life expectancy"], "It excludes migration."),
     ("Life expectancy is", "Average years a newborn can expect to live", ["The maximum age", "Death rate per 1000", "Fertility rate"], "A mean for a population."),
     ("In stage 2 of the model", "Death rate falls while birth rate stays high", ["Both are low", "Both are high and steady", "Birth rate is lower than death rate"], "Rapid growth."),
     ("A narrow-based pyramid suggests", "Falling birth rate and ageing", ["Very high fertility", "Large child population", "No elderly"], "Few children are born."),
     ("A high dependency ratio means", "Many dependants for each worker", ["Few children", "No old people", "High incomes"], "Burden on the working-age group.")],
    (dt_fig, "Birth and death rates through the four stages.", "Two curves: the death rate falls first, the birth rate falls later, with four labelled stages."),
    ["Statements on which stage sub-Saharan Africa occupies are general; check current syllabus phrasing.", "Dependency-ratio age bands (under 15, 15–64, 65+) are the common convention; confirm which the examiner uses."])

