L = lesson(ch, "migration", "Migration and urbanisation",
    ["Define migration and classify its types", "Explain push and pull factors and the effects of rural-urban migration", "Describe urban problems in African cities and ways of managing them"],
    [("definition", "Migration", "**Migration** is a permanent or semi-permanent change of residence. It can be **internal** or **international**, **voluntary** or **forced** (refugees, internally displaced people). **Push factors** drive people away (poverty, land shortage, insecurity, disasters); **pull factors** attract them (jobs, schools, services). **Obstacles** include cost, distance and lack of information."),
     ("retenir", "Urbanisation", "**Urbanisation** is the increase in the share of people living in towns. **Urban growth** is the rise in the number of town dwellers. Africa is urbanising rapidly because of rural-urban migration and natural increase in towns such as Douala and Yaoundé. Rapid growth outpaces housing, water, sanitation, transport and jobs."),
     ("retenir", "Urban problems and responses", "Typical problems: informal settlements with poor sanitation, traffic congestion, unemployment and a large informal sector, waste, flooding on low or built-over land, pressure on schools and health care. Responses: slum upgrading, planned housing, public transport, drainage, waste collection, and developing small and medium towns to share growth."),
     ("pieges", "Be precise", "- Do not call all migrants ‘refugees’: a refugee is forced across an international border.\n- Rural-urban migration also affects villages (loss of young workers, remittances).\n- Urban growth is not the same as urbanisation.")],
    [("Example 1 — Classify", "A young man leaves a village near Bafoussam to look for work in Douala. Classify the move and give one push and one pull factor.",
      ["He stays in the same country: internal migration.", "He chooses to go: voluntary.", "Push: few jobs in the village. Pull: more jobs in the port city."],
      "Voluntary internal rural-urban migration."),
     ("Example 2 — Effects on the village", "Give two effects of rural-urban migration on the village of origin.",
      ["Fewer young adults means less farm labour and an older population.", "Migrants may send money (remittances), which pays for school fees and housing.", "Fewer people can reduce pressure on land."],
      "Labour loss and ageing, but also remittances.")],
    [M("Which is a pull factor?", "Job opportunities in the city", ["Land shortage", "Drought", "Conflict"], "Pull factors attract migrants."),
     TF("A refugee is someone who moves voluntarily within their own country.", False, "Refugees are forced to cross an international border."),
     MT("Match each term to its meaning.", [("Push factor", "Reason to leave a place"), ("Internal migration", "Move within one country"), ("Informal sector", "Unregulated small-scale work"), ("Remittance", "Money sent home by a migrant")], "Standard migration terms."),
     M("Urbanisation means", "The rising share of the population living in towns", ["A fall in population", "Growth of villages", "A move from towns to villages"], "It is a percentage change."),
     O("Suggest two ways to reduce the problems of rapid urban growth in an African city and explain each.", "Slum upgrading with water, drainage and toilets improves health without moving everyone. Public transport and better roads reduce congestion and travel time. Developing smaller towns, with jobs and services, slows the flow to the largest cities. Waste collection reduces blocked drains and disease.",
       "Two measures (2); explanation of each (2)."),
     O("Discuss the causes and consequences of rural-urban migration in a developing country, using Cameroonian examples.", "Causes: poor incomes, land shortage and few services in villages push people out; jobs, schools and hospitals in Douala and Yaoundé pull them in. Consequences in towns: pressure on housing and services, informal settlements, unemployment, but also a labour supply and a larger market. In villages: labour shortage and an ageing population, though remittances help families. Balanced rural development is needed.",
       "Push and pull (2); urban consequences (2); rural consequences (1); local examples (1).")],
    [("An internal migrant", "Stays in the same country", ["Crosses a border", "Is always a refugee", "Is always forced"], "Internal means within a country."),
     ("An example of a push factor is", "Lack of land", ["Good schools", "Many jobs", "Electricity"], "Push factors drive people away."),
     ("A common problem of rapid urban growth is", "Informal settlements with poor services", ["Empty roads", "Too many farms", "No migrants"], "Services cannot keep up."),
     ("Urban growth refers to", "The increase in the number of town dwellers", ["The proportion of the population in towns", "Movement of towns", "Fall in birth rate"], "Number versus percentage."),
     ("Remittances are", "Money sent by migrants to relatives", ["Taxes paid by cities", "Aid from abroad only", "Bank loans"], "They support rural households.")],
    (pp_fig, "Push factors in the village and pull factors in the town.", "Two boxes joined by an arrow; the village box lists push factors and the town box lists pull factors."),
    ["Urban growth statements for Cameroon are qualitative; add current statistics only from official sources.", "Check whether the syllabus asks for named models of migration (for example Lee’s model)."])

fs_fig = boxes(["Availability: enough food is produced or imported", "Access: people can afford or reach it", "Utilisation: safe, nutritious food, clean water", "Stability: supply is reliable through the year"], 440, "blue", 12, 1, 32, 8)
L = lesson(ch, "agriculture", "Agriculture and food security",
    ["Distinguish types of farming and name the main crops of Cameroon’s regions", "Explain physical and human factors affecting farming", "Define food security and suggest ways to improve it"],
    [("definition", "Types of farming", "**Subsistence** farming feeds the family (cassava, plantain, maize, yams, groundnuts), often on small plots with shifting cultivation or bush fallow. **Commercial** farming sells the produce: plantations of banana, rubber, oil palm and tea; smallholder **cash crops** such as cocoa, coffee and cotton. **Pastoral** farming keeps cattle, mainly in the northern savanna and western highlands."),
     ("retenir", "Factors", "**Physical:** temperature, rainfall and its reliability, soil, relief and drainage. **Human:** markets and prices, transport, capital and credit, land tenure, technology, government policy, labour. Cocoa suits the humid south, coffee the cooler western highlands, cotton the drier north; volcanic soils help plantations near Mount Cameroon."),
     ("retenir", "Food security", "Food security means all people have enough safe, nutritious food at all times. It has four dimensions: **availability, access, utilisation, stability**. Threats: poor roads, post-harvest losses, climate variability, pests, conflict, low incomes. Responses: better seeds, storage, irrigation, farm roads, cooperatives, processing, and training."),
     ("attention", "Mistakes to avoid", "- Subsistence farming can include selling a small surplus: it is defined by the main purpose.\n- Yield = production ÷ area, not total production.\n- Food security is not only about producing more: access and storage matter.")],
    [("Example 1 — Yield", "A farmer harvests 3600 kg of maize from 2 hectares. Find the yield in kg per hectare, and the yield if the farmer’s neighbour gets 10 000 kg from 4 hectares.",
      ["Farmer: 3600 ÷ 2 = 1800 kg per hectare.", "Neighbour: 10 000 ÷ 4 = 2500 kg per hectare."], "1800 kg/ha and 2500 kg/ha."),
     ("Example 2 — Post-harvest losses", "A village loses a large part of its tomato crop on the way to Yaoundé. Suggest causes and solutions.",
      ["Causes: poor roads, no cooling or storage, long delays, careless handling.", "Solutions: improved roads, storage and drying, local processing into paste, collective transport."], "Better transport, storage and processing cut losses and improve access.")],
    [N("A farmer produces 4500 kg of cassava on 3 hectares. Yield in kg per hectare?", 1500, "4500 ÷ 3 = 1500.", 0, "kg/ha"),
     M("Which crop is typically grown in plantations near Mount Cameroon?", "Banana", ["Millet only", "Sugar beet", "Wheat"], "Banana plantations are well known in the south-west."),
     TF("Subsistence farming is mainly for family consumption.", True, "Its main purpose is to feed the family."),
     M("Which dimension of food security is about affordability?", "Access", ["Availability", "Stability", "Production"], "Access concerns income and distance."),
     MT("Match region and typical activity.", [("Humid south", "Cocoa growing"), ("Western highlands", "Coffee and food crops"), ("Northern savanna", "Cotton and cattle"), ("Coastal lowland", "Banana and oil-palm plantations")], "General patterns, not exclusive."),
     O("Explain three reasons why farmers in your region may not produce as much as they could, and suggest one remedy for each.", "Poor roads raise costs and spoil crops: build farm-to-market roads. Lack of credit prevents buying seed and fertiliser: microcredit or cooperatives. Unreliable rainfall damages harvests: irrigation, drought-resistant varieties. Pests and disease: training and affordable treatment.",
       "Three reasons (3); three remedies (3), maximum 4 marks overall with quality of explanation.")],
    [("Subsistence farming is mainly for", "Feeding the farmer’s family", ["Export", "Factories", "Tourism"], "Main purpose is consumption."),
     ("Yield is calculated as", "Production divided by area", ["Area divided by production", "Price times area", "Labour times rain"], "Output per unit of land."),
     ("Cocoa grows best in", "Hot, humid forest zones", ["Dry savanna", "Cold mountains", "Deserts"], "It needs warmth and rain."),
     ("Which is NOT a dimension of food security?", "Fashion", ["Availability", "Access", "Stability"], "The four dimensions are availability, access, utilisation, stability."),
     ("A way to reduce post-harvest losses is", "Better storage and roads", ["Planting later", "Burning fields", "Removing markets"], "Preservation and transport.")],
    (fs_fig, "The four dimensions of food security.", "Four stacked boxes naming availability, access, utilisation and stability."),
    ["Regional crop distribution is simplified: verify with the syllabus case studies.", "No production statistics are given; add recent figures only from official sources."])

