L = lesson(ch, "maps", "Map reading: scale, grid references, contours and gradient",
    ["Calculate distances from a map scale", "Give four- and six-figure grid references and bearings", "Read contours and calculate gradient"],
    [("definition", "Scale and distance", "A scale such as **1:50 000** means 1 cm on the map is 50 000 cm (0.5 km) on the ground. Distance = map length × scale denominator, then convert units: 100 000 cm = 1 km. A straight line is easy; for a curved road lay string along it and measure the string."),
     ("methode", "Grid references and bearings", "Give **eastings first** (along the corridor) then **northings** (up the stairs). A four-figure reference names a grid square; a six-figure reference adds tenths for precision. A **bearing** is measured clockwise from grid north, with three digits (e.g. 045° is north-east)."),
     ("retenir", "Contours and gradient", "**Contours** join points of equal height. Close together means steep; far apart means gentle; closed rings are hills or summits. **Gradient** = vertical interval ÷ horizontal distance, often written 1 in n. Convert both to the same unit first."),
     ("pieges", "Frequent mistakes", "- Reading northings before eastings.\n- Forgetting to convert cm to km.\n- Mixing up height difference and distance in the gradient fraction.\n- Taking bearings anticlockwise or from magnetic north.")],
    [("Example 1 — Distance", "Two villages are 6.4 cm apart on a map of scale 1:50 000. Find the real distance in km.",
      ["6.4 × 50 000 = 320 000 cm.", "320 000 ÷ 100 000 = 3.2 km."], "3.2 km."),
     ("Example 2 — Gradient", "A road climbs from 400 m to 500 m over a horizontal distance of 2 km. Find the gradient as 1 in n.",
      ["Vertical interval = 500 − 400 = 100 m.", "Horizontal distance = 2 km = 2000 m.", "Gradient = 100 ÷ 2000 = 1 in 20."], "1 in 20.")],
    [N("A map has scale 1:25 000. Two points are 8 cm apart. What is the real distance in km?", 2, "8 × 25 000 = 200 000 cm = 2 km.", 0, "km"),
     N("A slope rises 50 m over 1000 m. It is 1 in n. What is n?", 20, "1000 ÷ 50 = 20.", 0),
     M("What do closely spaced contours show?", "A steep slope", ["A gentle slope", "A river", "A road"], "Height changes quickly over a short distance."),
     M("In 4-figure grid references the first two digits give the", "Easting", ["Northing", "Height", "Bearing"], "Eastings come first."),
     TF("A bearing of 090° points east.", True, "Bearings are measured clockwise from north: 90° is east."),
     O("A map has scale 1:50 000. A path is 9 cm on the map and climbs 120 m. Calculate its real length and the average gradient (assume the map length is the horizontal distance).", "Length: 9 × 50 000 = 450 000 cm = 4.5 km. Horizontal distance 4500 m, rise 120 m: gradient = 120 ÷ 4500 = 1 in 37.5 (about 1 in 38).",
       "Distance (2), unit conversion (1), gradient fraction (1).")],
    [("A scale of 1:100 000 means 1 cm represents", "1 km", ["10 km", "100 m", "10 m"], "100 000 cm = 1 km."),
     ("A summit is shown by", "A small closed contour ring", ["A straight line", "A dotted line", "A wide blue area"], "Contours close around the highest point."),
     ("Bearings are measured from", "North, clockwise", ["East, anticlockwise", "South, clockwise", "West, anticlockwise"], "Three-figure bearings are clockwise from north."),
     ("Gradient 1 in 10 is", "Steeper than 1 in 50", ["Gentler than 1 in 50", "Flat", "Equal to 1 in 50"], "Smaller n means steeper."),
     ("In a six-figure reference the extra digits give", "Tenths of the grid square", ["Height", "Slope", "Date"], "They locate a point within the square.")],
    (cont_fig, "Concentric contours show a hill.", "Three concentric rings labelled 100, 200 and 300 metres around a hilltop."),
    ["The contour figure is schematic and not to scale.", "Confirm how many digits the syllabus expects in grid references and whether bearings use grid north."])

